response:
otice like “Data may be out‑of‑date because the server is unreachable.”
Why this is important
Prevents confusion – Users won’t think the app is working when it isn’t.
Avoids misleading information – No stale or incorrect numbers are presented as current.
Provides a path forward – A retry button lets the user recover once the backend is restarted.
Implementation tip (pseudo‑code)
async function fetchData() {
  try {
    setLoading(true);
    const resp = await api.get('/expenses'); // or any endpoint
    setData(resp.data);
    setError(null);
  } catch (err) {
    // Network error or non‑2xx response
    setError('Unable to connect to the server. Please check your network or try again later.');
    setData(null); // clear stale data
  } finally {
    setLoading(false);
  }
}


And in the render:

if (loading) return <Spinner />;
if (error)   return (
  <div className="alert alert-danger">
    {error}
    <button onClick={fetchData} className="btn btn-sm mt-2">Retry</button>
  </div>
);
return <ExpenseList data={data} />;


With this approach, when the backend is stopped the UI gracefully degrades to an informative error state rather than showing incorrect or missing information.

For testing only: FastAPI rejects the frontend origin due to CORS. What should be checked?

What to check when FastAPI blocks the frontend because of CORS

Item	Why it matters	How to verify / fix
CORS middleware is actually added	If the middleware isn’t installed, every request is rejected regardless of settings.	Look at the FastAPI app creation code: <br>from fastapi.middleware.cors import CORSMiddleware<br>app.add_middleware(CORSMiddleware, …)
allow_origins list	Determines which origins (scheme + host + port) are permitted.	Ensure the frontend’s origin (e.g., http://192.168.1.42:3000 or http://localhost:3000) appears in the list, or use ["*"] only during development.
allow_methods	Controls which HTTP verbs (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, etc.) are allowed.	Include the methods your frontend uses (["GET","POST","PUT","DELETE","OPTIONS"]) or ["*"].
allow_headers	Determines which request headers the browser may send (e.g., Authorization, Content-Type).	Add the headers you need (["*"] or specific ones like ["accept","content-type","authorization"]).
allow_credentials	Required if the frontend sends cookies or HTTP authentication.	Set to True only when you also list explicit origins (cannot use ["*"] with credentials).
Exposure of Access-Control-Allow-Origin header	The browser checks this header in the response.	After fixing the middleware, inspect the response (DevTools → Network → Headers) to see access-control-allow-origin: <your‑origin> (or *).
Preflight (OPTIONS) request handling	Browsers send an OPTIONS request before certain cross‑origin calls.	Verify that the OPTIONS endpoint returns 200 OK with the proper CORS headers; FastAPI’s middleware does this automatically when configured correctly.
Environment‑specific configuration	Sometimes dev vs. prod configs differ (e.g., behind a proxy).	Make sure the same middleware settings apply in the environment where the phone/laptop is testing.
Proxy or gateway in front of FastAPI	A reverse proxy (NGINX, Traefik, etc.) can strip or override CORS headers.	Check the proxy config to ensure it forwards the Access-Control-* headers unchanged or adds its own CORS rules.
Server logs	FastAPI will log when a request is rejected due to CORS.	Look at the console/output for messages like "Invalid CORS request"; they often mention the offending origin.
Quick checklist to resolve the issue
Add / verify middleware
from fastapi.middleware.cors import CORSMiddleware

app.add_middleware(
    CORSMiddleware,
    allow_origins=["http://192.168.1.42:3000"],   # or ["*"] for dev
    allow_credentials=True,
    allow_methods=["GET","POST","PUT","DELETE","OPTIONS"],
    allow_headers=["*"],
)

Restart the backend after changing the middleware.
Test from the phone: open the DevTools console/network tab and confirm the response includes access-control-allow-origin.
If you still see a block, double‑check that the origin string exactly matches (including scheme http/https and port) what the browser sends.

By ensuring the CORS middleware is present, correctly lists the frontend’s origin (or uses a safe wildcard in development), and allows the needed methods/headers—and confirming no intermediary is stripping the headers—the frontend will be allowed to communicate with the FastAPI backend.

For testing only: POST /api/v1/chat receives a malformed UUID or invalid date. What is a safe API response?

When the endpoint receives data that cannot be validated (e.g., a malformed UUID or an invalid date), the API should reject the request with a 422 Unprocessable Entity status and return a clear, machine‑readable error payload that tells the client what went wrong.

Example response

{
  "detail": [
    {
      "loc": ["body", "family_id"],
      "msg": "value is not a valid UUID",
      "type": "type_error.uuid"
    },
    {
      "loc": ["body", "expense_date"],
      "msg": "invalid date format; expected YYYY-MM-DD",
      "type": "value_error.date"
    }
  ]
}


Why this is safe

Correct HTTP status – 422 signals that the request was well‑formed but semantically invalid, avoiding confusion with 400 (bad request) or 500 (server error).
Structured payload – The detail array follows FastAPI’s default validation‑error format, making it easy for frontend code to parse and display field‑specific messages.
No sensitive data – The response contains only information about the validation failure; it does not leak internal state, stack traces, or business logic.
Idempotent & safe – Returning 422 does not modify any resources, so retrying after fixing the input is safe.

For testing only: PostgreSQL stops responding during a chat request. Should the agent claim a database write succeeded?

Loading...

Press Enter to send · Shift + Enter for a new line

Responses are based on information available to your family assistant.

UPDATE)	At the start of a transaction the service reads the commitment with SELECT * FROM commitments WHERE id = ? FOR UPDATE. This acquires a row‑level lock that blocks other transactions from reading the same row for update until the first transaction commits or rolls back. Subsequent UPDATE statements wait for the lock, serialize, and see the most recent state.	Concurrent updates that would interleave and produce an inconsistent intermediate state.
Serializable transaction isolation	Setting the transaction isolation level to SERIALIZABLE makes the database guarantee that the outcome of concurrent transactions is equivalent to some serial order. If a serialization anomaly is detected, the transaction is aborted and the application can retry.	More complex anomalies (e.g., read‑skew, write‑skew) that simpler locks might miss.
Idempotency key	The client supplies a unique identifier (UUID) with each mutating request. The server records the key together with the result of the operation. If a request with the same key arrives again (e.g., a retry), the server returns the previously stored response instead of re‑executing the logic.	Duplicate writes caused by client retries or network glitches.
Application‑level check‑then‑act with retry loop	After an optimistic‑lock failure, the service fetches the latest commitment, reapplies the client’s intended changes (or merges them), and retries the update a limited number of times.	Gives the user a smooth experience when conflicts are rare; the system converges on a consistent state without manual intervention.
Typical implementation flow (optimistic locking)

Read the commitment (including its version).

Send the update request containing the desired fields and the version you read.

Backend executes:

UPDATE commitments
SET amount = :amount,
    due_date = :due_date,
    priority = :priority,
    version = version + 1
WHERE id = :commitment_id
  AND version = :expected_version;


If the UPDATE affects 1 row, commit and return success.

If it affects 0 rows, return a 409 Conflict with a message like “The commitment has been modified by another request; please reload and try again.”

The client can then re‑fetch the latest commitment and repeat the operation.

Why this satisfies the safety rules
No invented state – The system only reports success when the database actually persisted the change (the UPDATE affected a row).
Never guess missing info – The version is supplied by the client; if it’s missing or wrong, the request is rejected rather than assuming a value.
Consistent with tool usage – All modifications go through update_commitment (or the underlying SQL it wraps), which can be instrumented to include the version check.
Clear feedback to the user – Conflict errors are user‑friendly and tell the user exactly what to do (reload and retry).

By combining optimistic locking (or a pessimistic lock/serializable isolation when appropriate) with idempotency keys for retries, the system eliminates lost updates and duplicate writes while keeping the API responsive and transparent to users.

For testing only: the API is reachable but the family selector returns an empty list. How should the UI distinguish this from loading or an API error?

How the UI can tell the three situations apart

Situation	What the UI should show	How to detect it in the code
Loading (request in flight)	A spinner / “Loading families…” indicator. No list is rendered yet.	loading === true (or equivalent state) while the promise is pending.
API error (network failure, 5xx, 4xx, etc.)	An error banner/message, e.g. “Unable to load families. Please check your connection or try again.” Often with a Retry button.	error !== null (or hasError === true) after the request settles, and loading === false.
Successful call but empty list (family selector returns [])	A helpful empty‑state view, e.g. “No families found. You can create a new family or contact support.” No spinner, no error banner.	loading === false, error === null, and families.length === 0.
Why this works
Loading is purely temporal – it ends as soon as the request finishes (success or failure).
Error indicates that something went wrong with the request itself; the UI should not interpret an empty array as an error.
Empty list means the request succeeded and the backend deliberately returned no data; the UI can therefore show a dedicated empty‑state UI.
Example (React‑like pseudo‑code)
const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);
const [error,   setError]   = useState(null);
const [families, setFamilies] = useState([]);

useEffect(() => {
  async function load() {
    setLoading(true);
    setError(null);
    try {
      const data = await api.getFamilies(); // → [] if none exist
      setFamilies(data);
    } catch (err) {
      setError(err.message || 'Failed to load families');
    } finally {
      setLoading(false);
    }
  }
  load();
}, []);

if (loading) return <Spinner />;

if (error) {
  return (
    <div className="alert alert-danger">
      {error}
      <button onClick={load} className="btn btn-sm mt-2">Retry</button>
    </div>
  );
}

// loading === false && error === null
if (families.length === 0) {
  return (
    <div className="empty-state">
      <p>No families found.</p>
      <button onClick={createFamily}>Create a family</button>
    </div>
  );
}

// normal rendering of the list
return <FamilyList families={families} />;


With these three mutually exclusive flags (loading, error, and the length of the data array), the UI can unambiguously differentiate between “still loading”, “something went wrong”, and “the backend returned an empty but valid list”. This prevents confusing a genuine empty state with a loading spinner or an error message.

